<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ramadan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ramadan is the ninth month of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamic-calendar">Islamic calendar</a>, observed by Muslims as a month of fasting, prayer and reflection. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate</a> profile, it was towards the end of Ramadan in 1121 that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-tumart">Ibn Tumart</a> proclaimed himself the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a>. This declaration began the movement that founded the Almohad Caliphate.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ramadan.jpg" alt="Ramadan" width="320" height="187" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gabby Canonizado from Dubai, United Arab Emirates · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Breaking_the_Fast_(2)_(27351979537).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Muslims hold that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> was revealed during Ramadan, and that Muhammad received his first revelation on Laylat al-Qadr, the Night of Power, generally believed to be an odd-numbered night in the last ten days of the month. They also hold that all earlier scriptures were revealed in this month. They were first commanded to fast in the second year of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hijra">Hijra</a>, 624 CE, but believe that fasting has always been necessary for believers to attain fear of God. Philip Jenkins argues that the fast grew out of the strict Lenten discipline of the Syrian Churches, a view other scholars share and some Muslim academics dispute.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamic-calendar">Islamic calendar</a> is lunar, and each month begins when the first crescent of the new moon is sighted. Since the calendar has no intercalation, Ramadan migrates through the seasons. It lasts 29 or 30 days. Many Muslims insist on a local sighting of the moon to mark the start, while others use the calculated time of the new moon or the declaration of Saudi Arabia, so dates differ between countries, usually by a day.</p><p>Fasting from dawn to sunset is obligatory for Muslims who have reached puberty and are not ill, travelling, elderly, breastfeeding, pregnant or menstruating. The predawn meal is the suhur and the meal that breaks the fast at sunset is the iftar, traditionally opened with dates in memory of Muhammad's practice. Muslims also abstain from sinful speech, devote more time to prayer and charity, and are encouraged to read the whole <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> over the month. The month ends with Eid al-Fitr, declared after the crescent of the next month is sighted or after 30 days of fasting.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Customs of the month vary widely. In some Islamic countries lanterns are strung up in public squares, a tradition believed to have originated under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a>, when the rule of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliph</a> al-Mu'izz li-Din <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a> was acclaimed by people holding lanterns. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> a mesaharati beats a drum through the neighbourhood to wake people for suhur. In Shia Islam the 19th to 21st days of the month are marked in mourning for Ali, the fourth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun</a> caliph, who was struck during morning prayer on the 19th day in 40 AH and died on the 21st. In the USSR the practice of Ramadan was suppressed by officials, and in Albania Ramadan festivities were banned during the communist period, though many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albanians">Albanians</a> fasted in secret.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ramadan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In <b>Ramadan</b> 1121 his followers proclaimed him the Mahdi, the divinely guided redeemer, and he later established his base at Tinmel in the High Atlas.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Islamic calendar counts years from Muhammad’s migration from Mecca to Medina in 622; because it is a lunar calendar, its months move through the seasons, and the fast of <b>Ramadan</b> comes about eleven days earlier each year.</blockquote><a href="/religion/islam">Islam · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Egyptian television adaptations premiered each <b>Ramadan</b> in the 1980s and 1990s, and an Egyptian radio version ran for 26 years.</blockquote><a href="/work/one-thousand-and-one-nights">One Thousand and One Nights · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>After the final Friday prayer of <b>Ramadan</b>, a mosque assembly in Arjona declared the town's independence from Ibn Hud and elected Muhammad ibn Yusuf as its leader.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-16">16 July in history</a></li></ul></section>
